   "title": "Note: Guide",
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## Breeze Voice Localize\n\nSame voice, another language. Localize takes a voice and returns that voice speaking a different language, so a campaign voice can cover every market without re-casting.\n\n### How to use\n\n1. Sign in with your ComfyUI account. This is an API node, so it bills per use.\n2. Feed **voice** from the Breeze Voice Selector, Voice Design or Voice Clone. This graph starts from an English voice.\n3. Set **language** to the target language (51 options, `English` by default; this graph uses `Spanish`).\n4. Queue the prompt. The node returns a new `voice` in the target language - plug it into Breeze Text to Speech to write fresh scripts in that language - plus the spoken `preview` saved here.\n5. Localize is also the fix for the one combination Text to Speech refuses: a voice reading text in a language it is not listed under. Localize the voice first, then use the localized voice for that language.\n\n### What stays and what changes\n\nTimbre, age and accent colour carry over; pronunciation and rhythm are rebuilt for the target language. If the result drifts too far from the original, localize from a cleaner source voice (noise-free, neutral delivery).\n"
   ],
   "widgets_values_named": {
    "text": "## Breeze Voice Localize\n\nSame voice, another language. Localize takes a voice and returns that voice speaking a different language, so a campaign voice can cover every market without re-casting.\n\n### How to use\n\n1. Sign in with your ComfyUI account. This is an API node, so it bills per use.\n2. Feed **voice** from the Breeze Voice Selector, Voice Design or Voice Clone. This graph starts from an English voice.\n3. Set **language** to the target language (51 options, `English` by default; this graph uses `Spanish`).\n4. Queue the prompt. The node returns a new `voice` in the target language - plug it into Breeze Text to Speech to write fresh scripts in that language - plus the spoken `preview` saved here.\n5. Localize is also the fix for the one combination Text to Speech refuses: a voice reading text in a language it is not listed under. Localize the voice first, then use the localized voice for that language.\n\n### What stays and what changes\n\nTimbre, age and accent colour carry over; pronunciation and rhythm are rebuilt for the target language. If the result drifts too far from the original, localize from a cleaner source voice (noise-free, neutral delivery).\n"
   }
  }
